    "text": "Question 1\n?\n\nConsider the following differential equation.\n𝑦\n″\n+\n𝑦\n′\n−\n12\n𝑦\n=\n14\n𝑥\n𝑒\n−\n4\n𝑥\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n𝑦\n(\n𝑥\n)\n=\n𝐴\n𝑒\n−\n4\n𝑥\n+\n𝐵\n𝑒\n3\n𝑥\n+\n𝑔\n(\n𝑥\n)\n,\nwhere \n𝐴\n and \n𝐵\n are constants. Which of the following could be the function \n𝑔\n(\n𝑥\n)\n?\n\nHint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):\n∫\n𝑥\n𝑒\n−\n7\n𝑥\nd\n𝑥\n=\n−\n1\n49\n(\n7\n𝑥\n+\n1\n)\n𝑒\n−\n7\n𝑥\n\na\n\t\n−\n(\n𝑥\n2\n+\n3\n𝑥\n7\n)\n𝑒\n−\n4\n𝑥\n\n\nb\n\t\n−\n(\n𝑥\n2\n+\n2\n𝑥\n5\n)\n𝑒\n−\n4\n𝑥\n\n\nc\n\t\n−\n(\n𝑥\n2\n+\n2\n𝑥\n7\n)\n𝑒\n−\n4\n𝑥\n\n\nd\n\t\n−\n(\n𝑥\n2\n+\n𝑥\n3\n)\n𝑒\n−\n4\n𝑥\n\n\ne\n\t\n−\n(\n𝑥\n2\n+\n𝑥\n7\n)\n𝑒\n−\n4\n𝑥\nSubmit",
